[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m First, we compute \(9!\) which is \(362880\). The prime factorization of \(9!\) is \(2^7 \times 3^4 \times 5 \times 7\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m We need to find the sum of the positive divisors of \(9!\) that have a units digit of 1. This means we are looking for divisors \(d\) such that \(d \equiv 1 \pmod{10}\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m To solve this, we need the divisors of \(9!\) to satisfy \(d \equiv 1 \pmod{10}\). This requires \(d\) to be congruent to 1 modulo 2 and 5. 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m 1. \(d\) must be odd, so the exponent of 2 in its prime factorization must be 0. Hence, \(a = 0\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m 2. The remaining part of the divisor must be \(\equiv 1 \pmod{2}\) and \(\equiv 1 \pmod{5}\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m Given \(9! = 2^7 \times 3^4 \times 5 \times 7\), we need \(3^b \times 5^0 \times 7^e \equiv 1 \pmod{5}\). This simplifies to \(3^b \times 2^e \equiv 1 \pmod{5}\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m Checking the possible values of \(b\) and \(e\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=75873)[0m 
